\item \textbf{The correlation-selection gap.}
$\rho{=}0.38$ on the primary test bed (up to 0.45 on a ceiling-effect control) coexists with selection $\Delta{\approx}0$ against 13--18~pp oracle headroom. Log-probability---the only per-sample signal---is too compressed within instances to discriminate (median range 0.041 nats, pooled within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){=}0.248$); instance-level signals, even when converted to per-sample consensus-proximity rankings (MBR), do not improve selection despite positive within-instance utility correlation. Single-metric evaluation risks selecting the wrong signal.

\item \textbf{Diagnostic ablations.}
A 4-seed free-form decoding ablation confirms NER compression is not caused by constrained decoding; entity-token LP increases within-instance variance ($4{\times}$) but worsens selection, pointing to intrinsic model overconfidence on entity tokens. Verbalized confidence is even more compressed, confirming compression is not LP-specific (Appendix~\ref{app:verbalized_confidence}).

\item \textbf{Difficulty- and task-dependent signal rankings.}
Rankings shift with task complexity and collapse at the capability boundary.

